now over three million men from the  Commonwealth served in the First World  War one and a half million came from pre  partition India 74 thousand of them lost  their lives and 11 were awarded the  Victoria Cross well early this morning  wreaths were laid at the Delhi War  Memorial and among those taking part in  a ceremony was the chair of the Foreign  Affairs Select Committee and the former  Army officer Tom ticket who joins us  hello thank you for being with us this  morning good morning Sophie how are you  I'm all right thank you I'm good now I  just mentioned in the introduction to  your interview that 1.5 million men from  South Asia signed up to the British Army  in World War one which is an incredible  number of people really who Muslims  Sikhs Hindus do you think we do enough  to remember their sacrifice no I don't  think we do enough and we've got to  remember these men who came from every  village from every community in the then  undivided India communities that are now  of course in India and Pakistan and  Bangladesh and Sri Lanka came as  volunteers they volunteered like other  people who joined our armed forces in  those days they joined out of a sense of  duty out of a sense of honour and and of  course you know it's but young men for  the spirit of adventure and very much  the similar reasons that people join our  armed forces today and so I think it's  right that we do much more to remember  them and remember that at moments like  the very early days in 1914 it was the  Indians who held the line when the  Germans that he broke through to cut off  the Channel ports they actually say  these are men who volunteered and and  yet they were under colonial rule do you  think the government should apologise to  Commonwealth leaders for some of the  wrongs of the past well I certainly  think we should apologize for some of  the wrongs that happen and Prime  Minister David Cameron did apologize for  some of the wrongs that happened but for  all of us it's important to recognize  that there were wrongs but it's there's  also a lot that we've done together and  it's impossible today to talk about  British history without speaking of  India and it's impossible today to speak  of Indian history without speaking of  Britain you know we really are two  countries that have  grown-ups together over the past two  centuries and I think it's very  important that we remember that we wrote  the stories together and with  cooperation we can help shape the future  together as well talking about shaping  that future do you think there is an  opportunity with breath set to reshape  relationships with being kind I  absolutely do I think it's extremely  important that we used this moment to  look hard at our relationships around  the world and none more so than our  relationship with India but I've been  struck over the few days that I've been  here at the warmth of the reception that  I've received from members of parliament  from civic society from people from all  walks of life and the proximity of our  relationship now we don't agree on  everything that would be too much to  expect with any country but we have a  very very deep common understanding of  the world as we see it we share very  strongly common values we share the  values of the rule of law of democracy  of a free press that are not found in as  many countries as we would like and  certainly here in the indo-pacific  region are absolutely vital to our  future and so I look forward I hope to  working very much more closely with the  people of India at every level now  yesterday Donald Trump missed a visit to  a war cemetery because of bad weather  and I just want to read you what cynical  assumes the Conservative MP in the  ground some of Winston Churchill said  about that he said they died with their  face to the phone and that pathetic  inadequate Donald Trump couldn't even  defy the weather to pay his respects to  the fallen he's not fit to represent  this great country do you agree well  I'll leave Nicholas to speak for himself  but he has a certain way with words all  I'll say is I spent four years in  Afghanistan and I don't remember  operations being canceled when it rained  I don't remember them being canceled for  the Col I don't remember us not refusing  to soldier because the weather was  inclement I seem to remember that in  Iraq and Afghanistan when sometimes it  was minus 15 and sometimes it was 50  degrees in the shade we sold it on how  does your experience in Iraq and  Afghanistan shape the politics because  you've spoken about you know some pretty  horrific things that happened a suicide  bomber for example blown themselves up  in there  courtyard of your office in Helmand I  mean this must have been an incredibly  shaping its Varun's view okay all of us  who've served have had the privilege of  meeting some of the most impressive men  and women that you'll ever likely to  meet and for me that's what shaped my  politics it's that sense of duty that I  have to our country and to the people  who've given so much you know and it's  recognizing that I mean Sophie I I've  been blessed but come from an incredibly  strong and loving family and I've been  had the good fortune of economic  stability  all my life now I know that many of the  men and women I had the privilege to  serve with did not have that and what  I've got to do is to try and make sure  as a politician that the benefits of our  society that the good things in our  society has spread as widely as possible  and that's exactly what I'm trying to do  through the politics that I now practice  now that's where for me this is a  continuation of a sense of duty let's  talk about that sense of duty you said  of your time in the Army in Iraq I was  one of many soldiers who questioned the  decision but obeyed the orders and I  just wonder if that will be you know go  take on brexit as well and how are you  feeling about the deal that Theresa May  is currently fighting for is that  something that you can support well it's  very hard to know what the deal is I'm  afraid I unlike the two or perhaps three  people who are really in the room  negotiating I'm afraid none of us  actually know what the prime minister is  going to bring forward so until I know  what the details are I find it very  difficult to comment I'm just commenting  in the dark and that's why I have been  very careful not to say too much on it  because I just be guessing and what the  people deserve now you know people of  our country deserve now is not more  guesses from politicians as to what the  future might hold but serious considered  thought as to what the options are  before us we've got a pretty good idea  that haven't we of the kind of thing  that Theresa May is looking at  particularly with regards to the  backstop the whole of the UK staying in  a customs union with the European Union  in an effective customs union Oh Joe  Johnson your colleague resigned this  week and he said that there's been a  failure of British statecraft  on a scale on theme' since the Suez  Crisis  is he right well Joe again has his own  ways of expressing things and there  isn't a Johnson in the land who doesn't  have a particularly colorful way of  making their point clear so I'll leave  him to speak for himself but I think  it's again I'm friend I'm gonna be  difficult in now on this because I don't  know what the Prime Minister has got as  the final negotiations and what the back  spot does of course matter the idea of  the backstop is that it never is used so  depending on what the Prime Minister  brings forward I will then know whether  or not the backstop is important or not  so I'm waiting now to hear what the  Prime Minister has to say and Joe is  right in that this is an extremely  important moment for the United Kingdom  this is a moment when we have decided to  reset the relationship for the first  time in 40 years to really change the  strategy of the United Kingdom from one  a proximity trade to one of remoter  trade and now that's that's a really big  call but we did discuss this for quite a  long time before the referendum in 2016  this was a topic of much discussion  around the country as we all remember  and many of us were very clear in the  way that we considered it best to to  speak about the questions and I had  these debates in 2015 and I had them in  2016 and so that I'm afraid this isn't a  huge surprise to me that the questions  now before us are very difficult okay I  can see that you're not going to be too  drawn on brexit and you've previously  said that after breaks it's time for a  generational shift in the Conservative  Party what did you mean by that  well what I meant by that is I've got a  huge privilege to sit alongside some  really impressive guys on on the  conservative benches you know people  like Vickie Atkins who's in the home  office at the moment is really one of  the brightest and most able people of  our generation Kip bobhouse who's doing  amazing things with housing and doing it  completely brilliantly remembering the  sense of place and identity that we all  need in  in it and then of course you know I can  run through people like James cleverly  and Johnny Mercer and so on who who  really have fantastic ways of expressing  and communicating so whether it's you  know whether it's that generation of  Rishi's tunic and Lucy Fraser or whether  it's another generation just after us of  people like Palmerston I just think  they're ahead of an opportunity on the  conservative benches to really have some  serious impact on some serious ideas to  take the country forward and someone  said to me that the list of people who  aren't running for the Conservative  Party leadership will end up being  longer than the list of people who are  running for the Conservative Party  leader yeah exactly and I wonder if it's  something that you'd consider putting  your name ahead at the moment I'm just  waiting to see what the deal the Prime  Minister brings back I mean I haven't  made any great secret of the fact that I  would I would consider running from my  party leadership at some point but for  me the whole point of this is not to run  for the leadership is to change the  country for me the whole point of this  is not to be an individual to be a team  you know if one thing the soldiering  taught me is that individuals we don't  achieve very much but we have an amazing  conservative team you know I've listed  some names there and there are many  others and if we get the right people in  the team then actually the leadership  becomes not academic but certainly only  only an expression of that and that's  what we've got to get to we've got to  get a really strong conservative team  forward arguing for the future of the  country arguing for a positive future  arguing for a future that includes  everybody in our community and really  values the work that so many do and you  know from the young to the old you know  we have an amazingly dynamic society  sometimes I think we forget that and and  it's a great shame because it leads us  to despondency where when actually we  should be fully optimistic and so I'm  really focused on bringing you know  doing my bit to support a conservative  team and see where that gets us it's  interesting to listen to you talking  about the future there and the concepts  have been in power for eight years do  you sometimes worry that the new current  administration the current form of the  Conservative Party is a bit tired a  little bit consumed by brexit well I  think it's the nature of any major  struggle that it consumes those who are  engaged in it I mean we know this  whether you look at a merger  an acquisition or any sort of you know  major operation and military or whatever  it it you always find that those who  have really focused in on it who really  had to do nothing but that for a long  period of time tend to get a bit worn  out not hardly surprising but it's it's  a credit to those who are engaged in the  debate at the moment that they are  devoting every inch of energy every  every moment of energy in order to to  try and bring forward the best outcome  for the British people and I think  that's that's absolutely right now once  that is done will people look for a  change I suspect they will and I think  it's it's for the Conservative Party to  come forward and be that change and I  think we can be I think we have an  amazing opportunity as I've listed  already you know from the from the  number of people and ideas on our  benefit to come forward and make a  fantastic change and bring the country  forward  ok Tonto you can have thank you

